# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH — every env var for the whole stack lives in
# this one file. Copy it to `.env` and fill in real values. There are
# NO per-profile `.env` files; compose maps these vars
# into each service as needed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── LLM Provider API Key (VPS-ONLY secret — never committed) ──────
# Real key lives ONLY in the git-ignored root `.env` on the VPS.
# It belongs to T3/opencode on the host (the agent no longer runs in a
# container), and the kept MCP servers read only the two Mongo variables.
# Provider + model are selected in T3/opencode (the app has no model
# picker since #493). This file carries a placeholder only.
OPENCODE_API_KEY=sk-...

# ── Android app password (single credential for sync) ──────────────
# PASSWORD is the bearer credential health-api accepts and the Android
# app sends on every call. Must match the Password field in Agento
# Settings. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
PASSWORD=change-me

# ── Single app URL (reverse proxy for sync) ─────────────────────────
# The proxy routes /api/* + /health → health-api :8000. Point the Agento
# app's Server URL here: http://<host>:8080. Direct health-api
# (:8001 — compose default; HEALTH_HOST_PORT remaps the host side) stays
# published for comparison.
# APP_PORT=8080                   # override (compose default: 8080)
# HEALTH_HOST_PORT=8001           # override (compose default: 8001)

# ── Remote MongoDB (Atlas: money/health/cookbook domain data) ───────
# Read by ./scripts/mcp-run.sh for each Go MCP server (T3 supplies no
# environment of its own, so the launcher parses this file; a variable
# inherited from whatever shell started T3 still passes through). For the MCP
# launchers only, a value already exported in the environment wins — that is
# how a dev session points at a throwaway database. The orchestrator scripts
# are the opposite: ./scripts/hermes.sh, retention.sh and sysmon.sh load with
# `load_root_env`, where this file wins, so exporting a throwaway MONGODB_URI
# does not redirect compose, a retention run or sysmon.
MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://user:pass@cluster0.example.mongodb.net/
MONGODB_DB=hermes

# ── Google Workspace (Gmail) MCP server (#515) ─────────────────────
# Read-only Gmail for the agent: search + read message content, nothing
# else. The server is the upstream Python package
# taylorwilsdon/google_workspace_mcp (MIT), installed into the
# git-ignored .workspace-mcp/venv by scripts/build-workspace-mcp.sh and
# launched by scripts/workspace-mcp.sh. Procedure, re-consent and the
# headless first-run steps: documentation.md → "Google Workspace (Gmail)".
#
# Create a Google Cloud project with the Gmail API enabled, then an OAuth
# client of type "Desktop app" (a loopback redirect URI needs no
# registration). Paste its id + secret here — these two are secrets and
# live only in the git-ignored root .env.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-...
# The one mailbox the agent reads. Also used as the consent `login_hint`,
# so the browser opens on the right account.
USER_GOOGLE_EMAIL=you@example.com
# OAuth loopback callback port (default 8000). Consent redirects to
# http://localhost:<port>/oauth2callback, so a desktop OAuth client needs
# no registered URI; the headless flow SSH-forwards exactly this port.
# Read by scripts/workspace-mcp.sh (parsed from this file — T3 supplies no
# environment of its own, though an exported value wins over this one), so an
# edit needs the MCP servers to respawn.
# WORKSPACE_MCP_PORT=8000
# Where the refresh tokens are cached (default .workspace-mcp/credentials/,
# git-ignored). Override only to point at a store outside this checkout, and
# give an ABSOLUTE path: this file is parsed, not sourced, so ~ and $HOME are
# NOT expanded (the launcher refuses such a value instead of creating a
# literal "~" directory). scripts/workspace-mcp.sh reads it from here too, so
# the re-consent cleanup in documentation.md deletes from the same place the
# server uses.
# WORKSPACE_MCP_CREDENTIALS_DIR=
